    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my monstera deliciosa has been getting these weird yellow leaves lately. most of them are near the bottom of the plant. i think i've been watering it maybe once a week? not sure if i should water less or if it needs more food or something. any idea what's up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a classic sign of your Monstera being a bit too \"wet\" for its own good, which is exactly what happens when you forget to check the soil moisture before a **watering schedule** that's too rigid. Since you mentioned watering once a week, that's likely the culprit, especially if your pot doesn't have drainage holes or if the soil is a bit heavy. The yellowing at the bottom is the plant's way of saying, \"Hey, I'm drowning, and I'm recycling my nutrients because I can't breathe!\"\n\nYou don't need to add more food right now; in fact, over-fertilizing a stressed plant will just make the situation worse, like feeding a starving person who is actually full of poison. Instead, try lifting the pot to feel the weight of the soil; if it feels heavy and spongy, it's time to let it dry out completely before the next **garden visit**.\n\nOnce you've let the soil dry out for a few days, give it a gentle drink, but be careful not to let the water pool at the bottom of the pot, as that's a recipe for root rot. If the leaves stay yellow, you might need to trim the",
      "source": "target",
